DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
mem
- physical memory device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Char major 2, minor 0.
.bd
/dev/mem
reads and writes the machine's full 24-bit physical address space.
A seek sets the physical address of a transfer, and a read or write
advances it.

Below the first megabyte the driver serves the space a 4K page at a
time through the map window, and cracks a transfer at the page
boundary.  Above the first megabyte the bank has to be selected, and
the task register that selects it also moves the kernel out from
under itself.  The driver therefore installs a small trampoline in
the CPU board's own page, which no bank switch can move, and moves
128 bytes per call through the map window and a bounce buffer there.
A larger transfer is cracked into 128-byte pieces, and one that
crosses a 4K page boundary is cracked there too.  A read leaves each
piece in the bounce for the caller's copy; a write copies the
caller's data into the bounce and then the trampoline stores it.

/dev/mem addresses the S-100 bus, not the CPU board's own page.  The
map windows the driver uses are at 0x1000 and 0x2000, above the page
the board serves locally, so physical address 0 is the first page of
S-100 memory, whatever the board holds at virtual 0.  The board's 1K
of RAM, its registers and its EPROM are the kernel's low page; they
are what
.bd
/dev/kmem
(kmem (4)) reports, and /dev/mem cannot reach them.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
